A||||6||Tom88gt||6.5 subwoofer in stock location?||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||06-18-2001||10:56 PM||||Has anyone modified their factory subwoofer enclosure to accept a 6.5 inch sub?  If it can easily be done, it would be preferable to the stock 5 inch size.  <P>Granted, the 5 inch speaker is an easy drop in.  Several good speakers are available for about $20.  But none really stand out as a good choice for a subwoofer.  <P>The local stereo shop has a very nice 5.25 Kicker mid-bass driver for $80/pr.  Best 5.25 speaker I've seen yet.  Very well built with a huge magnet.  If I go this route, I can probably sell the second speaker for 1/2.<P>What other 5 inch speakers have been used with good results (besides Rodney's MCM brand speaker or the Radio Shack one). ||||reg||5
Z||000001||cadaver||06-19-2001||03:03 AM||||I put an 8 inch in the stock location<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/003744.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/003744.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||06-19-2001||10:19 AM||||Thats not what he ment, he wants to know if he can USE the factory sub encloser to put a 5.6" speaker in   (ie. make the hole bigger to except the speaker)      <P>I have no clue, I do not own a factory sub, wish i did, i have never even seen one so i hope someone can answer your question||||reg||1
Z||000003||Toddster||06-19-2001||01:15 PM||||I read something in the Fiero Focus about some guy in Wisconsin who re-cones factory original subwoofers. Brings them up to new condition. Hey, it's an option:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fierofocus.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierofocus.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tom88gt||06-19-2001||04:12 PM||||Cadaver...I've seen your posts on your 8 inch install.  Very nice.  But I have a factory enclosure that I would like to use, just with a bigger speaker.<P>Rebuilding the crappy factory speaker is a waste of time and $.  It does not have the magnet structureor power handling capacity for bass.  Plus, I'm going to use a 50-100 watt rms amp.  Way more than the 15 watt factory amp.<P>I recall seeing some posts about people whop installed a 6.5 inch sub in the factory enclosure.  I can't find any discussions in the archives. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||06-19-2001||04:55 PM||||I dont think much more than the 5 1/4 will fit  the box is pretty small.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||06-19-2001||05:15 PM||||You would have to have to make a custom fiberglass adapter. It would stick the speaker down about 1.5" more then it is now. <P>Reconeing of the factory speaker is a hugh waste. You can buy Rodney's replacement speaker for like $35 and it is far better then the factory one.<P>Those Kicker midbass units are descent, but there not subwoofers! Midbass band extends from 80hz to 400hz. Subbass is 80hz and below. The factory speaker, and Rodney's replacement are actual subwoofer's, not midbass. You won't get as much bass from a Kicker midbass as you would with Rodney's replacment. Cars have naural cabin gain for midbass, thats why you don't see them hardly anywere. My system in progess will have CDT 3-way component speakers in it, with have a 6.5" midbass, 4" midrange, and 1" tweeter. So it will have a heck of alot of midbass. I would not reccomend useing a midbass in the factory subwoofer location. Also, a midbass driver will want a sealed box, and factory box is ported and too large.||||reg||1
